title: Content hashes kept fresh
tags: Sync [[File fingerprint]]
kind: reference
summary: Files marked with mf_hash keep their full hash current — recomputed in the background after each change, computed by reconcile or mf hash when missing — and sync compares two fresh hashes instead of the bytes, seeing a same-second rewrite the stamp misses.

Decided by the user on 10 November 2026. A file's full hash
(`mfr_full_hash`, [[File fingerprint]]) is otherwise computed only when a duplicate
scan or an orphan's search needs it, and the watcher's `Modify` drops it. Some files are
worth more: their hash kept current lets sync tell two contents apart without
reading them, and see a rewrite their stamp does not show.

!! Marking

<<cmd "mf_hash">> (`bool`) marks the files whose hash is kept fresh. Like `mf_watch` and
`mf_sync` it is inherited down the `mfr_path` tree: the nearest ancestor that sets
it wins, so one directory marks everything under it and a subdirectory set to
`false` opts out. It is an `mf_*` field ([[Reserved fields]]): written without
`force`, and synced, so the two sides of a link agree on it.

!! Computing it

* <<cmd "mf hash">> `QUERY` computes the partial and the full hash of each file the query selects, marked or not, reusing a stored hash whose stamp still matches ([[The duplicate hash cache]]); `--rehash` ignores them.
* The watcher hands a marked file to a ''background worker'' when it creates its record, or on a `Modify`. When the size or mtime moved, it clears the hashes first, as for any file. When they did not, it keeps them — an echo must not destroy them ([[Watcher pitfalls]]) — and the worker reads the file anyway: that is the same-second, same-size rewrite the stamp misses. The worker hashes with the store free and writes the hashes with their stamp only when the file's `stat` did not move while it read, and is the stat its record holds — a record that has not caught up with its file is left to the event that will hand the file over again — and only where they differ: an unchanged file moves no version. Until then a changed file has no hash, and sync compares bytes.
* Re-reading a record's file (`POST …/refresh`, which sync runs after each file it writes, [[Refreshing a record]]) hands it over too.
* The queue is in memory, like the watcher's buffer: a restart loses it, and reconcile hands the files over again.
* Reconcile, once its write is committed, hands over every marked file of its scope that has no fresh hash (below) — never hashing under its own lock.

!! Fresh

A stored full hash is ''fresh'' when its stamp (`mfr_hash_mtime`, `mfr_hash_size`)
matches the record's `mfr_mtime` and `mfr_size`. Freshness is judged on the record,
as every stamp of sync is ([[Change detection in sync]]).

!! What sync does with it

* The snapshot keeps, per side, the fresh full hash it agreed on, beside the stamp.
* A side's content changed when its stamp moved, ''or'' when it holds a fresh hash that differs from the one its side of the snapshot holds.
* Two sides holding a fresh hash are equal when the hashes are, and no byte is read; otherwise the bytes are compared. A snapshot from before this has no hash: the stamp alone decides, then the bytes.
* Freshness is exact because a `datetime` crosses the wire to the millisecond ([[Value types]]): at the second, a hash taken before a rewrite of the same second would pass for the new content's.
* After a run copies a marked file, the worker writes the destination's hash, which moves its record's version once more: the next plan finds nothing to do for that link but record it again.

An xxHash3-64 collision between two different contents is the accepted risk of
[[Why duplicates are not compared byte by byte]].

Without the watcher — a subtree whose watching is off, a daemon that was not running —
nothing hands the file over: the stored hash stays trusted on its mtime and size,
and a same-second, same-size rewrite stays unseen, as it does for duplicates.
